# =============================================================================
# scriptum — plain and numbered holes in one literal
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Plain holes (`§`, `§{name}`) count from 0 in their own order and ignore
#     numbered holes (`§N`).
#   • A literal that mixes the two still runs; the checker reports WARN035
#     `mixed_plain_numbered_template_holes`, names the argument each plain
#     hole reads (`§=§0`), and suggests numbering every hole.
#   • The numbering does not change: `"§0 and §"("a")` is "a and a" and
#     `"§ § §0"("a", "b")` is "a b a".
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting the plain hole to continue after the highest number — it
#     starts again at 0.
#
# See also: scriptum, named-holes, template-repeated-hole
# =============================================================================

# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   a and a
#   a b a

main {
    print "§0 and §"("a")
    print "§ § §0"("a", "b")
}
